|~ `::tagged`, the kind of every value under a tag, and the verbs of
   tagged values. Each resides on `::qlang/tagged`, which the walk of a
   tagged value reaches after its own tags. ~|

::tagged
  |~~ The kind of every value under a tag, `::Box[1 2]`, `::Box{:a 1}`,
      whatever the payload: the walk from a tagged value to its verb
      passes its own tags, then this kind, then the kind of its payload's
      shape. The verbs that take a tag apart live here, `payload`, which
      answers the value beneath, and `within`, which edits it and puts
      the tag back through its constructor. A value is put under a tag
      by a literal, `::T<payload>`, a step of its own kind, or by `tag`.
      ::link~(::tag | doc) ::link~(::construct | doc)
      ::link~(::qlang | doc :values)

      Every value under a tag reaches the verbs of this kind:
      ~(::Box {} | ::Box[1 2] | payload | eq [1 2])
      The step that builds one is no value of this kind:
      ~(~(::Box[1 2]) | first | type | eq ::construct)
  ~~|

:payload
  |~~ The value the tag holds, the reverse of `tag`: `value | tag ::Foo
      | payload | eq value` holds of every value. A set's payload is its
      vector.

      ~(::Box {} | ::Box[42] | payload | eq [42])
      ~(::Pair {} | ::Pair[1 2] | payload | eq [1 2])
      ~(::Note {} | ::Note"hello" | payload | eq "hello")
      ~(#[2 1] | payload | eq [1 2])
      The declaration of a kind is a map under `::builtin`, taken apart
      the same way:
      ~(::vec | spec | payload | keys | has :verbs)
  ~~|
  ::verb~(::builtin{:impl :qlang/prim/payload})

::PayloadSubjectNotTaggedInstanceError
  |~~ `payload` takes a value under a tag; a value of a kind of the
      core, a number or a plain map, stands under none.
      ~(
        42 | payload
        !| [type /actualType]
        |  eq [::PayloadSubjectNotTaggedInstanceError ::number]
       )
  ~~|

:within
  |~~ An edit under one tag: `tagged | within ~(…)` runs the quote
      against the payload as `apply` runs it, a fork whose declarations
      stay inside, and the answer returns the subject's kind, minted
      back under its tag, whose constructor runs once, at the rewrap.
      The steps between may break the tag's invariant,
      since an invariant holds of the result: a set's constructor
      normalizes the vector the edit answers, where a record's refuses
      it. An error the edit answers passes as it is, and a deeper stack
      of tags is reached by nesting `within`.

      ~(#[3 1] | within ~([/0 /1 /0]) | eq #[1 3])
      ~(::Box {} | ::Box[1 2] | within ~(reverse) | eq ::Box[2 1])
      ~(::Box {} | ::Box#[3 1] | within ~(within ~([/1 /0 2])) | eq ::Box#[1 2 3])
  ~~|
  ::verb~(:code ::quote | :returns / | ::builtin{:impl :qlang/prim/within})

::WithinSubjectNotTaggedInstanceError
  |~~ `within` edits the value beneath a tag and puts the tag back; a
      value under no tag is edited by `apply`.
      ~(
        [1 2] | within ~(reverse)
        !| [type /actualType]
        |  eq [::WithinSubjectNotTaggedInstanceError ::vec]
       )
  ~~|

::WithinCodeNotQuoteError
  |~~ The edit `within` runs is a quote, `within ~(reverse)`; a value
      computed at the call is refused, the error a step answered there
      among them.
      ~(
        #[3 1] | within 1
        !| [type /actualType]
        |  eq [::WithinCodeNotQuoteError ::number]
       )
  ~~|
